 the exact class of vulnerability intent.md and this change exist to close (an unattended Bash call reaching a guardrail file while the path-based gate sees nothing). They are reachable in CI with the allow-listed Bash(git *); the only remaining layer there is plugin/ci/settings.ci.json's sandbox.filesystem.denyWrite (also added by this diff), but spec.md's own Flagged concerns state a by-hand run, native Windows, or any host with no sandbox has 'the hook alone' -- on such a host both bypasses are complete and silent.; No test in the diff or in tests/test_hooks.py exercises a git invocation with a global option before the subcommand, or an inner-shell call with a flag between the interpreter and its -c/-Command token: tests/test_hooks.py's existing git -c coverage (line 625, 'git -c user.name=x commit -m y') belongs to test_plan_sync_detects_commit_in_any_position, a different hook, not shell_guard; the shell_guard wrapper/inner-shell test (test_shell_write_targets_sees_through_wrappers_and_inner_shells, line 1980) only exercises the bare 'bash -c'/'sh -c'/'cmd /c'/'pwsh -Command' pairs with no extra flag. plan.md's own named residual ('an unlisted wrapper, content decoded and piped to an interpreter') is a different shape from a documented git/shell flag shifting a recognised idiom's argument position by one slot -- this was missed, not weighed and accepted.; This diff itself carries the evidence of the gap: changes/0002-security-authz-the-guardrail-self-protec/evidence/adversarial-review-c.json (committed at an earlier HEAD, ec837ed, in this same diff) already found and described both bugs in detail. The only code commit in this round after that review, 3a90bd0, fixed solely the third, unrelated finding in the same report (git -C's directory leaking into a later chain part -- confirmed below, correctly fixed). The git-verb and inner-shell argument-position bugs were left unaddressed: fix-requests.json shows empty requests/not_applied, and verifier.md's scope for this round is explicitly 're-verification of the git -C chain-leak fix (commit 3a90bd0)' only -- it does not re-examine or close the other two findings from the same prior review.; Verified separately that the git -C chain-leak finding is correctly fixed at this HEAD: _resolution_walk's git -C branch (lines 416-430) now computes a part-local base/base_nl and no longer reassigns the shared current/nonliteral the rest of the chain loop reads, matching the new tests test_shell_guard_git_dash_c_does_not_leak_into_a_later_chain_part and test_shell_guard_resolves_a_git_dash_c_target and verifier.md's live subprocess runs. That part of the fix is sound and is not grounds for escalation by itself; the escalation rests on the other two, still-open gaps from the same review round.",
