++      "body": "Request changes on PR #152 (build(0002)). Gate (c) parked on the capped run's wall clock, not on the code; this round resets the clock and settles the reviewer's open `escalate` and a fresh-context review of the diff. Fix them in the files plan.md already lists, with a test each; no change to spec.md (choice 164), plan.md only where a step or the file list changes.\n\n1. `plugin/hooks/shell_guard.py` `_part_targets`, the git branch: the subcommand is read at a fixed position (`words[1]`) with no flag-skipping, so a git global option before the verb hides the write \u2014 `git -c core.fileMode=false rm CLAUDE.md`, `git --git-dir=.git rm CLAUDE.md`, `git --work-tree=. checkout -- CLAUDE.md` all yield no candidate (`shell_write_targets` returns `[]`; `git rm CLAUDE.md` returns `['CLAUDE.md']`). Skip git's global options before the verb the way `cp`/`mv`/`rm` skip flags (`_nonflag`), consuming the argument of `-c`, `-C`, `--git-dir`, `--work-tree`, `--namespace` and `--exec-path` when it is a separate token; a test per form, and the same forms in the `-C` resolution path (`_git_dash_c`), which must still see `-C <dir>` when another global option precedes it.\n2. `_inner_shell_command`: only the adjacent pair (`bash`, `-c`) is recognised, so an interpreter flag between the program and `-c` skips the recursion \u2014 `bash --norc -c 'echo x > CLAUDE.md'`, `sh --posix -c '\u2026'`, `pwsh -NoProfile -Command '\u2026'`, `cmd /d /c '\u2026'` are never parsed inside, while `bash -c 'echo x > CLAUDE.md'` yields `CLAUDE.md`. Find the `-c`/`/c`/`-Command` token after any flags, recurse into the next token; a test per interpreter.\n\n3. `_part_targets` and `decide()`: a directory target never matches a protected pattern (`/.claude/**` needs a slash after `.claude`; `docs/policy.md` does not match `docs`), so a recursive delete or move of a guardrail directory, or of an ancestor of a `protected_paths` entry, is allowed \u2014 `python -c \"import shutil; shutil.rmtree('.claude')\"`, `git rm -r -q .claude`, `git rm -r -q docs`, `Remove-Item -Recurse -Force .claude` all `allow` (reproduced on a scratch project with `docs/policy.md` under `protected_paths`); `shutil.rmtree` is on the spec's own idiom list. Deny a resolved target that is an ancestor of a protected pattern's fixed prefix or of the plugin root; a test with the four forms.\n4. The two-argument interpreter calls check the source, not the destination: `shutil.copy('README.md','CLAUDE.md')` yields the candidate `README.md` and is allowed; the same for `os.replace`, `os.rename`, `shutil.move`, node `copyFileSync`/`renameSync`/`cpSync` and `[IO.File]::Copy`/`Move`. Take the last literal (the destination), as the `cp`/`mv` branch already does; a parametrized row per call.\n5. Python `open` write forms the regex misses, each `[]`: `open('CLAUDE.md', mode='w')`, a mode in a variable (`m='w'; open('CLAUDE.md', m)` \u2014 fail closed), `open(r'CLAUDE.md','w')`, `Path('CLAUDE.md').open('w')`, `os.open('CLAUDE.md', os.O_WRONLY|os.O_TRUNC)`. One idiom row each.\n6. PowerShell paths are mangled by the POSIX backslash escape: with `tool_name: PowerShell`, `Set-Content .\\CLAUDE.md x` becomes the candidate `.CLAUDE.md` and is allowed, `Set-Content 'CLAUDE.md' x` is denied, and an unquoted `C:\\\u2026\\CLAUDE.md` loses every backslash. `.\\path` is the ordinary PowerShell spelling on the owner's platform. Keep `\\` literal when the tool is PowerShell (its escape is the backtick); a test `test_shell_guard_reads_a_backslash_path_on_powershell`.\n7. `_powershell_targets` takes the wrong positional when a named parameter precedes the path: `Set-Content -Value x CLAUDE.md` \u2192 `x`, `\"x\" | Out-File -Encoding utf8 CLAUDE.md` \u2192 `utf8`, `Set-Content -Path:CLAUDE.md -Value x` (the colon form is not parsed), `New-Item -ItemType File CLAUDE.md` \u2192 `File` \u2014 all allowed. Skip the value of every `-Name value` pair when looking for the positional, split `-Param:value`; the four ro