+      "body": "Request changes on PR #152 (build(0002)). Gate (c) parked on the capped run's wall clock, not on the code; this round resets the clock and settles the reviewer's open `escalate` and a fresh-context review of the diff. Fix them in the files plan.md already lists, with a test each; no change to spec.md (choice 164), plan.md only where a step or the file list changes.\n\n1. `plugin/hooks/shell_guard.py` `_part_targets`, the git branch: the subcommand is read at a fixed position (`words[1]`) with no flag-skipping, so a git global option before the verb hides the write \u2014 `git -c core.fileMode=false rm CLAUDE.md`, `git --git-dir=.git rm CLAUDE.md`, `git --work-tree=. checkout -- CLAUDE.md` all yield no candidate (`shell_write_targets` returns `[]`; `git rm CLAUDE.md` returns `['CLAUDE.md']`). Skip git's global options before the verb the way `cp`/`mv`/`rm` skip flags (`_nonflag`), consuming the argument of `-c`, `-C`, `--git-dir`, `--work-tree`, `--namespace` and `--exec-path` when it is a separate token; a test per form, and the same forms in the `-C` resolution path (`_git_dash_c`), which must still see `-C <dir>` when another global option precedes it.\n2. `_inner_shell_command`: only the adjacent pair (`bash`, `-c`) is recognised, so an interpreter flag between the program and `-c` skips the recursion \u2014 `bash --norc -c 'echo x > CLAUDE.md'`, `sh --posix -c '\u2026'`, `pwsh -NoProfile -Command '\u2026'`, `cmd /d /c '\u2026'` are never parsed inside, while `bash -c 'echo x > CLAUDE.md'` yields `CLAUDE.md`. Find the `-c`/`/c`/`-Command` token after any flags, recurse into the next token; a test per interpreter.\n\n3. `_part_targets` and `decide()`: a directory target never matches a protected pattern (`/.claude/**` needs a slash after `.claude`; `docs/policy.md` does not match `docs`), so a recursive delete or move of a guardrail directory, or of an ancestor of a `protected_paths` entry, is allowed \u2014 `python -c \"import shutil; shutil.rmtree('.claude')\"`, `git rm -r -q .claude`, `git rm -r -q docs`, `Remove-Item -Recurse -Force .claude` all `allow` (reproduced on a scratch project with `docs/policy.md` under `protected_paths`); `shutil.rmtree` is on the spec's own idiom list. Deny a resolved target that is an ancestor of a protected pattern's fixed prefix or of the plugin root; a test with the four forms.\n4. The two-argument interpreter calls check the source, not the destination: `shutil.copy('README.md','CLAUDE.md')` yields the candidate `README.md` and is allowed; the same for `os.replace`, `os.rename`, `shutil.move`, node `copyFileSync`/`renameSync`/`cpSync` and `[IO.File]::Copy`/`Move`. Take the last literal (the destination), as the `cp`/`mv` branch already does; a parametrized row per call.\n5. Python `open` write forms the regex misses, each `[]`: `open('CLAUDE.md', mode='w')`, a mode in a variable (`m='w'; open('CLAUDE.md', m)` \u2014 fail closed), `open(r'CLAUDE.md','w')`, `Path('CLAUDE.md').open('w')`, `os.open('CLAUDE.md', os.O_WRONLY|os.O_TRUNC)`. One idiom row each.\n6. PowerShell paths are mangled by the POSIX backslash escape: with `tool_name: PowerShell`, `Set-Content .\\CLAUDE.md x` becomes the candidate `.CLAUDE.md` and is allowed, `Set-Content 'CLAUDE.md' x` is denied, and an unquoted `C:\\\u2026\\CLAUDE.md` loses every backslash. `.\\path` is the ordinary PowerShell spelling on the owner's platform. Keep `\\` literal when the tool is PowerShell (its escape is the backtick); a test `test_shell_guard_reads_a_backslash_path_on_powershell`.\n7. `_powershell_targets` takes the wrong positional when a named parameter precedes the path: `Set-Content -Value x CLAUDE.md` \u2192 `x`, `\"x\" | Out-File -Encoding utf8 CLAUDE.md` \u2192 `utf8`, `Set-Content -Path:CLAUDE.md -Value x` (the colon form is not parsed), `New-Item -ItemType File CLAUDE.md` \u2192 `File` \u2014 all allowed. Skip the value of every `-Name value` pair when looking for the positional, split `-Param:value`; the four rows.\n8. The directory-change parser takes `words[1]` as the directory even when it is a flag or a special word: `cd -P .claude && python -c 1 > settings.json` resolves under `<root>/-P/` and is allowed (the write lands in `.claude/settings.json`); `cd docs && cd - && echo x > CLAUDE.md` is allowed; `Set-Location -Path .claude; Set-Content settings.json x` allowed; `sl` is not in `_CD_WORDS`; `pushd \u2026 && popd && \u2026` resolves to the wrong directory. Skip flags; add `sl`, `chdir`, `Push-Location`; fail closed on `cd -`, a bare `cd`, `popd` and `Pop-Location`; and add the `pushd` and `Set-Location` resolution tests spec.md Acceptance names (today absent \u2014 which is how this went unseen).\n9. Shell structure hides the head word, so the git, PowerShell and `cp`-style branches never fire: `git status & git rm -q CLAUDE.md` (a lone `&` is not a separator), `(git rm -q CLAUDE.md)`, `{ git rm -q CLAUDE.md; }`, `if true; then git rm -q CLAUDE.md; fi`, `GIT_TRACE=0 git rm -q CLAUDE.md` (an assignment prefix), `(cd .claude && python -c 1 > settings.json)` \u2014 all `[]`, all allowed, each confirmed in bash to delete or write the file. Split on a lone `&`; strip a leading `(`, `{`, `!`, `then`, `do`, `else` and `NAME=value` words and a trailing `)`; a test `test_shell_write_targets_sees_through_subshells_groups_and_assignments`.\n10. `_strip_heredocs` matches `<<WORD` anywhere in a line, inside quotes and in a here-string, and then drops every later line: `git log -1 --format='<<EOF'` followed on the next line by `git rm -q CLAUDE.md` yields `[]`, and `python -c 1 <<< \"x\"` followed by the same does too (`<<<` read as `<<\"x\"`); bash ran the second line. Detect heredocs in the quote-aware tokenizer and exclude `<<<`; a test that line 2's `git rm CLAUDE.md` is still seen in both forms.\n11. The non-literal rule misses brace expansion and git pathspec magic: `git rm -q {CLAUDE,README}.md` is allowed (bash removed both files), `git rm -q ':(icase)claude.md'` and `':/CLAUDE.md'` are allowed (git removed `CLAUDE.md`). Add `{` to `_NONLITERAL_RE` and fail closed on a git path argument that starts with `:`; the two fail-closed rows.\n12. `>&WORD` is not parsed as a file redirect: `python -c 1 >&CLAUDE.md` gives the candidate `&CLAUDE.md` and is allowed (bash wrote the file). Parse `>&WORD` as a redirect when WORD is neither a digit nor `-`; an idiom row.\n13. Options of the allowed programs that write a named file are not idioms, so in CI \u2014 where the hook's surface is exactly these programs \u2014 each is allowed: `git diff --output=CLAUDE.md` (wrote a 117-byte diff into it), `git checkout main CLAUDE.md` (no `--`), `git config --file sdlc.yaml a.b c`, `python -m json.tool x.json sdlc.yaml` (wrote it), `python -m pytest --basetemp=.claude` (deleted `.claude/settings.json`), `gh release download \u2026 -O CLAUDE.md`. Add them as idioms with one row each, plus a negative row proving the framework's own `git diff --stat --patch --output=changes/<id>-<slug>/evidence/diff-c.patch` stays allowed (it resolves under `changes/`).\n14. Git Bash absolute paths (`/c/Users/\u2026/CLAUDE.md`) fall outside a `C:/\u2026` root (`rel_to` returns `None`) and are allowed while the `C:/` spelling is denied \u2014 read in the code, not reproduced here (needs a drive-letter root). Map `/x/\u2026` to `x:/\u2026` in `_resolve` when the root has a drive letter; a test with root `C:/p` and target `/c/p/CLAUDE.md`.\n15. **A false denial that parks ordinary runs**: the interpreter-literal scan reads every part's raw text, quotes included, so `git commit -m \"deny open('CLAUDE.md','w') via Bash\"`, `gh pr comment 144 --body \"\u2026 fs.writeFileSync('sdlc.yaml','x') \u2026\"` and `grep -n \"open('CLAUDE.md','w')\" tests/test_hooks.py` are each denied with the \"Protected path \u2026 Stop and report\" reason (reproduced: `BLOCK`) \u2014 this change's own commit messages, fix responses and review comments quote exactly these strings. Scan only the code argument of `python -c`/`python3 -c`/`node -e`/`pwsh -Command`; add the three commands to `test_shell_guard_allows_ordinary_commands`. The same for a `#` comment (`git status # then > CLAUDE.md` is denied): strip an unquoted comment.\n16. PowerShell `$null` fails closed as a non-literal target: `git fetch origin 2>$null` and `python -m pytest > $null` with `tool_name: PowerShell` are denied (\"not a literal path\") \u2014 the PowerShell spelling of `> /dev/null`, which the spec's negative list allows. Treat `$null` as the null device; the two rows on the allowed list.\n17. `tests/test_ci.py` (the one pre-existing assertion the plan permits to change, decision 1 of the third design review): `data[\"sandbox\"] == source[\"sandbox\"]` became equality on `network` plus the `denyWrite` list, so `enabled`, `failIfUnavailable` and `allowUnsandboxedCommands` are no longer asserted on the rendered copy (reproduced: a copy with the three keys dropped passes the updated assertions). Restore full equality with only `filesystem.denyWrite` substituted: `data[\"sandbox\"] == {**source[\"sandbox\"], \"filesystem\": {\"denyWrite\": [...]}}`.\n\n**Nits, for the same round where cheap** (the reviewer's reading, not reproduced here):\n\n18. `decide()` looks the test-file lock up on every shell command, even with no write candidate, and fails closed when it cannot read the change folder \u2014 on a `sdlc/<id>/c` branch of a project whose change folder is missing, `git status` is denied. Look the lock up only when there is at least one candidate; a test.\n19. A denial is logged before it is emitted: with a hung git the log's own git call makes a block take 40 s against the hook's 30 s timeout, and a timed-out PreToolUse hook does not block (NOTES \u00a78). Emit first, or pass `default_path` so the log needs no git call.\n20. `_deny_write_entries` (`plugin/ci/run_phase.py`): a non-list `protected_paths` (`protected_paths: docs/x.md`) is iterated character by character and yields the project roo